{
    "word": "qubit",
    "lang_code": "en",
    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "9211976a-e1ef-3f1c-a9b0-677fabed2745",
                "word": "qubit",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "090b344c-41fa-4805-82f1-f985ed315d53",
                        "entry_id": "9211976a-e1ef-3f1c-a9b0-677fabed2745",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "qubits"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "81afcd22-df34-3514-a930-e82752cbeac6",
                        "entry_id": "9211976a-e1ef-3f1c-a9b0-677fabed2745",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A quantum bit; the basic unit of quantum information described by a superposition of two states; a quantum bit in a quantum computer capable of being in a state of superposition; A binary qudit."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "577b524b-7497-43f9-bba0-5fab1b8510cc",
                                "sense_id": "81afcd22-df34-3514-a930-e82752cbeac6",
                                "text": "Quantum computing, on the other hand, is based on quantum bits, or qubits."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9c6774fd-4a65-415f-b38b-8c990717f2f7",
                                "sense_id": "81afcd22-df34-3514-a930-e82752cbeac6",
                                "text": "Each extra qubit in a quantum machine doubles the number of simultaneous operations it can perform."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "47305532-e136-4dd6-850d-98daff5a66a7",
                                "sense_id": "81afcd22-df34-3514-a930-e82752cbeac6",
                                "text": "Google’s Sycamore computer has all of 53 qubits to its name, as does a new IBM computer, installed online at the company’s Quantum Computation Center in Poughkeepsie, N.Y. System One, IBM’s black cube from tomorrow, only has 20 qubits."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a5bfec2c-f85c-4514-9c5f-54f72c80fae4",
                                "sense_id": "81afcd22-df34-3514-a930-e82752cbeac6",
                                "text": "By harnessing that odd behavior, scientists can instead build a quantum bit, or qubit, which stores a combination of 1 and 0. Two qubits can hold four values at once. And as the number of qubits grows, a quantum computer becomes exponentially more powerful."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "c23d5558-dd6c-4f9b-a601-90052d32a4ed",
                        "source_entry_id": "9211976a-e1ef-3f1c-a9b0-677fabed2745",
                        "source_sense_id": "81afcd22-df34-3514-a930-e82752cbeac6",
                        "source_sense_description": "quantum bit",
                        "target_lang_word": "qubit",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "qubit",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "424b4f54-0d77-308c-902f-dc392693a00b",
                                        "entry_id": "52a50f6a-f9c1-3bd7-9b8d-5ec94a052a76",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Unité élémentaire de stockage d’information quantique."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "112c7d31-99a5-47b3-9a85-ea34d56015a4",
                                                "sense_id": "424b4f54-0d77-308c-902f-dc392693a00b",
                                                "text": "Un bit quantique (qubit) peut quant à lui se trouver dans plusieurs états simultanément."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e6d02b16-1931-4411-b35d-b20263720488",
                                                "sense_id": "424b4f54-0d77-308c-902f-dc392693a00b",
                                                "text": "Alors que, dans un ordinateur lambda, les informations sont codées sous la forme de bits de valeur 0 ou 1, les bits quantiques peuvent dans le même temps prendre les valeurs 0 et 1. Plus impressionnant, ces qubits peuvent interagir."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "b6ee6d5b-d475-48fa-af89-b9e27b3dc60e",
                        "source_entry_id": "9211976a-e1ef-3f1c-a9b0-677fabed2745",
                        "source_sense_id": "81afcd22-df34-3514-a930-e82752cbeac6",
                        "source_sense_description": "quantum bit",
                        "target_lang_word": "Qubit",
                        "target_lang_code": "de",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Qubit",
                                "target_lang_code": "de",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "625fd3c9-d748-3713-bcc9-7b3dbd1c0c53",
                                        "entry_id": "02fe9740-2a26-393b-b358-1dc0a70fce3c",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Maßeinheit für die kleinste auf Quantencomputern darstellbare Informationseinheit"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e3aa554d-a696-4742-99bd-63a75edc951c",
                                                "sense_id": "625fd3c9-d748-3713-bcc9-7b3dbd1c0c53",
                                                "text": "„Die dort agierenden Quantenbits, die ‚Qubits‘ genannt wurden, agierten bei einer Temperatur von rund -273 Grad Celsius, also nahe dem absoluten Nullpunkt.“"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "145de644-0ac8-47d4-aa96-222a18e6d2fd",
                                                "sense_id": "625fd3c9-d748-3713-bcc9-7b3dbd1c0c53",
                                                "text": "„Quantencomputer dagegen arbeiten mit Qubits, sie können 0 und 1 zugleich darstellen, genauso wie sich in der Quantenwelt ein Elektron an zwei Orten gleichzeitig befinden kann.“"
                                            }
                                        ],
                                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
